---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Two corrections of record, both verified against the paper's full text:
//  1. There is no separate "NT2" publication. The NT-v2 models (50M-500M params, 12 kb context,
//     rotary embeddings, swiGLU) are introduced in THIS paper -- 18 "NT-v2" mentions in it.
//     The previous version of this page listed "NT2 (BioNeMo 2024): 500M params" as its own work.
//  2. The citation said "Nature Methods Vol 21, pp. 2523-2535". Crossref: volume 22, pages
//     287-297, issue 2, online 28 Nov 2024. The old page range was the DOI suffix (02523) reused.
// NTv3 is a genuinely separate paper and now has its own page at /papers/nucleotide-transformer-v3/.
// Checked by `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug nucleotide-transformer`.
const title = "Nucleotide Transformer: Building and Evaluating Robust Foundation Models for Human Genomics";
const description = "Technical summary of the Nucleotide Transformer (Dalla-Torre et al., InstaDeep, Nature Methods 2025): DNA foundation models from 50M to 2.5B parameters pretrained on 3,202 human and 850 multispecies genomes, evaluated by probing and fine-tuning across 18 tasks.";
const readingTime = "24 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">DNA Language Models & Foundation Models</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Nature Methods · online 28 November 2024</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Hugo Dalla-Torre*, Liam Gonzalez*, Javier Mendoza-Revilla*, Nicolas Lopez Carranza, Adam Henryk Grzywaczewski, Francesco Oteri, Christian Dallago, Evan Trop, Hassan Sirelkhatim, Guillaume Richard, Michael Skwark, Karim Beguir, Marie Lopez, Thomas Pierrot*</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> InstaDeep, London, UK; NVIDIA; Technical University of Munich</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-024-02523-z" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1038/s41592-024-02523-z</a> · Nature Methods 22(2), 287–297</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      Protein language models showed that self-supervised pretraining on sequence alone yields
      representations that transfer. The <strong>Nucleotide Transformer</strong> asks whether the same holds
      for DNA, and does so as a controlled study rather than a single model: transformers from
      <strong>50 million to 2.5 billion parameters</strong>, pretrained on <strong>3,202 human genomes</strong>
      and <strong>850 genomes from diverse species</strong>, evaluated by both <strong>probing and
      fine-tuning</strong> across <strong>18 genomic tasks</strong>. Its central finding is about data, not
      scale: the <strong>Multispecies 2.5B</strong> model <strong>outperforms or matches</strong> the
      same-sized model trained on 3,202 human genomes — on human tasks.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The Question: Does Pretraining Transfer to DNA?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#models">2. The Four Pretraining Corpora</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tokenization">3. Six-Mer Tokenization and the 6 kb Ceiling</a></li>
        <li><a href="#evaluation">4. Probing versus Fine-Tuning</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">5. The Multispecies Result</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ntv2">6. NT-v2: What This Paper Actually Introduces Second</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & Where the Line Runs to NTv3</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The Question: Does Pretraining Transfer to DNA?</h2>
      <p>
        Protein language models trained by masked-residue prediction on large sequence datasets compete with
        and outperform earlier approaches on downstream tasks through transfer learning. DNA presents both
        <strong>an opportunity and a challenge</strong>: intricate patterns of natural variability across
        species and populations are readily available, but the signal is sparse in a way protein sequence is
        not.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper's contribution is as much benchmark as model. It positions itself as a
        <strong>benchmark of foundational genomics models</strong> that should <strong>serve as a reference
        for the development of further language models in genomics</strong> — which is why the ablation over
        pretraining corpora matters more than any single checkpoint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="models" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. The Four Pretraining Corpora</h2>
      <p>
        The controlled comparison holds architecture roughly fixed and varies what the model reads:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Model</th>
              <th>Parameters</th>
              <th>Pretraining corpus</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Human ref 500M</strong></td>
              <td>500 million</td>
              <td>The single human reference genome</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1000G 500M</strong></td>
              <td>500 million</td>
              <td>3,202 genetically diverse human genomes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>1000G 2.5B</strong></td>
              <td>2.5 billion</td>
              <td>3,202 genetically diverse human genomes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Multispecies 2.5B</strong></td>
              <td>2.5 billion</td>
              <td>850 genomes from diverse species</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        For scale context, the paper notes the largest model is <strong>20 times larger than DNABERT-2</strong>
        and <strong>ten times larger than the Enformer backbone</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tokenization" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. Six-Mer Tokenization and the 6 kb Ceiling</h2>
      <p>
        The models use <strong>six-mer tokens</strong> with a maximum of <strong>1,000 tokens</strong>, chosen
        as <strong>a trade-off between sequence length (up to 6 kb) and embedding size</strong>, and because
        six-mers <strong>achieved the highest performance when compared to other tokenizations</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> The constraint that shapes everything downstream
        </div>
        <p>
          A 6 kb receptive field is a promoter and its immediate surroundings. It is not an enhancer 100 kb
          away, and it is not a topological domain. Every result in this paper is therefore about local
          sequence grammar — which is exactly the limitation that the NTv3 successor, with its 1 Mb context,
          exists to remove.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The tokenization is also used as an analysis tool: the authors compute
        <strong>six-mer token probabilities, by masking each token in turn, for every 6-kb window in
        chromosome 22</strong>, and read the result as a metric of sequence constraint and importance learned
        by the model — a self-supervised analogue of evolutionary conservation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="evaluation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Probing versus Fine-Tuning</h2>
      <p>
        Models are evaluated through <strong>two different techniques: probing and fine-tuning</strong>.
        <strong>Probing</strong> uses the frozen learned embeddings as input features to a small downstream
        model; <strong>fine-tuning</strong> updates the transformer's own weights on the task.
      </p>
      <p>
        Running both is a methodological point rather than a formality. Probing measures what the
        representation already contains; fine-tuning measures what the initialisation makes reachable. A model
        can be poor under probing and excellent after fine-tuning, and reporting only one hides which is
        happening.
      </p>
      <p>
        The comparison baseline is strong by construction: the <strong>BPNet convolutional architecture</strong>
        trained from scratch on each of the <strong>18 tasks</strong>, described as
        <strong>widely used in genomics and a very strong default architecture</strong> — not a weak
        baseline chosen to flatter the transformer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper also emphasises that the models <strong>can be fine-tuned at low cost</strong>, and yield
        accurate predictions <strong>even in low-data settings</strong>, which is the practical argument for a
        foundation model in a field where labelled data is the binding constraint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. The Multispecies Result</h2>
      <p>
        The finding the paper leads with is that <strong>training on a diverse dataset, represented by the
        Multispecies 2.5B model, outperforms or matches the 1000G 2.5B model</strong> on several tasks — and
        that it does so on <strong>human</strong> prediction tasks, having never concentrated on human
        sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper's reading is that <strong>transformer models trained on diverse species have learned to
        capture genomic patterns across species</strong>, enabling better generalization in human-based
        prediction tasks. Cross-species variation supplies a signal that within-species human variation does
        not: 3,202 human genomes differ at a small fraction of positions, whereas 850 species differ
        everywhere that function permits — which makes the invariants visible.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Why this is the transferable lesson
        </div>
        <p>
          Both 2.5B models have the same capacity and the same objective. The only difference is the corpus,
          and the more <em>evolutionarily</em> diverse corpus wins on the target species. Choosing pretraining
          data by evolutionary breadth rather than by raw volume is a design principle that generalises well
          past this paper — and the authors say as much, anticipating that
          <strong>future studies may benefit</strong> from it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The models also learned, <strong>despite no supervision, to focus attention on key genomic
        elements</strong>, and their zero-shot scores can be used to
        <strong>improve the prioritization of pathogenic variants</strong> — with the paper noting that
        prioritization based on multispecies sequences performed best, consistent with severely pathogenic
        variants tending to fall in regions conserved across species.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ntv2" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. NT-v2: What This Paper Actually Introduces Second</h2>
      <p>
        A point worth stating plainly, because it is easy to get wrong: <strong>the NT-v2 models are
        introduced in this paper</strong>. They are not a separate publication.
      </p>
      <p>
        The authors developed <strong>four new NT models (NT-v2) with parameter counts ranging from 50 million
        to 500 million</strong>, incorporating a series of architectural changes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rotary embeddings</strong> in place of the original positional scheme</li>
        <li><strong>swiGLU activations</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Elimination of MLP biases and dropout mechanisms</strong>, in line with contemporary language-model practice</li>
        <li><strong>Context length expanded to 12 kb</strong>, to accommodate longer sequences and capture more distant genomic interactions</li>
        <li><strong>Extended training duration</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The direction of travel is the interesting part: the v2 models are <em>smaller</em> than the 2.5B v1
        model while performing competitively, with the gains coming from architecture, context and training
        length rather than parameters.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation</h2>

      <h3>The pretraining objective</h3>
      <p>
        Masked language modelling over six-mer tokens. For a sequence of tokens
        <Katex tex="x = (x_1, \dots, x_T)" /> with masked positions <Katex tex="\mathcal{M}" />:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLM}} = -\sum_{i \in \mathcal{M}} \log p_{\theta}\big(x_i \mid x_{\setminus \mathcal{M}}\big), \qquad x_i \in \{A,C,G,T\}^{6}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        With six-mer tokens the vocabulary is <Katex tex="4^6 = 4096" /> plus special tokens, and a
        1,000-token window spans 6,000 bases.
      </p>

      <h3>Constraint from token probability</h3>
      <p>
        Masking each token in turn and reading its probability gives a per-window score the paper interprets
        as learned sequence constraint:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="c(i) = \log p_{\theta}\big(x_i \mid x_{\setminus i}\big)" />
      </div>
      <p>
        This is the same construction that produces zero-shot variant scores — the log-likelihood a model
        assigns to what is actually there, used as a proxy for how much the sequence is allowed to change.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & Where the Line Runs to NTv3</h2>

      <h3>Resolution and range</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Six-mer tokens are not single bases.</strong> A tokenizer that consumes six nucleotides at a time cannot natively express a single-nucleotide event at arbitrary offset; variant scoring inherits that granularity.</li>
        <li><strong>6 kb, or 12 kb for NT-v2, is short.</strong> Distal regulation operates over hundreds of kilobases, and no amount of parameter scaling fixes a receptive field.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What "foundation model" is and is not established here</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Evaluation is 18 tasks, not the genome.</strong> The tasks are the ones with labels, which skews toward well-studied element classes.</li>
        <li><strong>Attention on key genomic elements is an observation, not a mechanism.</strong> That attention concentrates on functional sequence is evidence the representation is meaningful; it is not an explanation of any individual prediction.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Successor
        </div>
        <p>
          The long-range successor — <strong>NTv3</strong>, a U-Net-style architecture with single-base
          tokenization and context up to 1 Mb, jointly trained on sequence and function — is summarised
          separately at
          <a href="/papers/nucleotide-transformer-v3/" style="color: var(--color-accent);">/papers/nucleotide-transformer-v3/</a>.
          It is a different paper with a different corpus, and the two should not be described as one model
          family with shared numbers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
